 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Short answer:** Victorian women's job market has softened over the past year. Unemployment is up, although it is still close to the historical norm, and employment has been roughly flat.\n\n**Victoria, women (seasonally adjusted, latest is September 2026):**\n- **Unemployment rate: 5.1%.** This is up from 4.1% in September 2025 and a low of about 3.9% in April 2025. The rise has been steady since early 2026, reaching a peak of 5.2% in July 2026.\n- **Employed: about 1.84 million.** That is only about 5,000 more than in September 2025, so most of the job growth from 2025 has been lost. The number was 1.79 million in January 2025.\n- **Participation rate: 63.6%.** This is little changed over the year.\n- **Employment-to-population ratio: 60.4%.** This is down from about 61.3% a year earlier.\n\n**Comparison with all Victorians (September 2026):**\n- Unemployment is 5.1% for women and 5.05% for all persons, so the gap has closed. A year earlier women were slightly below the overall rate (4.1% vs 4.75%).\n- Women's participation (63.6%) remains about 4 points below the overall rate (67.4%), a gap that has been fairly stable.\n\nMonthly survey estimates for a state's female labour force are noisy, so single-month moves of a few tenths of a point shouldn't be overread. The direction over 2026 is clear, though: unemployment is rising and employment is stalling.",
